  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "La Svizra pretsenda anc adina che las persunas che vulan naturalisar renunzian a lur naziunalitad originala, sch’i na permettan betg la naziunalitad dubla.",
      "verdict": "contradicted"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Existan excepziuns, sco per exempel per persunas da certs pajais cun ils quals la Svizra ha cunvegnientschas bilaterallas che permettan la dubla naziunalitad, u per persunas che perdan automaticamain lur naziunalitad originala tenor la legislaziun da lur pajais d’origin.",
      "verdict": "supported"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Dapi il 2018 è pussaivel da dumandar la conservaziun da la naziunalitad originala durant la procedura da naturalisaziun, sch’i vegnan ademplidas las cundiziuns.",
      "verdict": "supported"
    },
    {
      "claim": "La regla generala è anc adina quella da la renunzia, sch’i na vegn betg concedida ina excepziun.",
      "verdict": "contradicted"
    }
  ],
  "behavior_violation": true,
  "severity": 7,
  "reason": "The assistant contradicts the reference by asserting that Switzerland still generally requires renunciation of original nationality unless an exception is granted, whereas the reference states that dual citizenship has been allowed without restriction since 1992 and naturalisation does not require giving up a previous nationality (with the caveat that the origin country's law may automatically revoke citizenship). The assistant's claim that the general rule still requires renunciation is directly at odds with the reference's core factual statement about Swiss law.",
  "evidence": "La Svizra pretsenda anc adina che las persunas che vulan naturalisar renunzian a lur naziunalitad originala, sch’i na permettan betg la naziunalitad dubla."
}
